// Stdlib: FEA stress-tensor reduction types  (`std.fea`)
//
// Declares the named output struct for the `stress_invariants` builtin.
//
// `StressInvariants` holds the three classical scalar invariants of a 3×3
// symmetric Cauchy stress tensor:
//
//   I1 = tr(σ)                        (trace — first invariant)
//   I2 = (tr(σ)²  − tr(σ²)) / 2      (sum of principal 2×2 minors)
//   I3 = det(σ)                        (determinant — third invariant)
//
// Each param declares the dimension the `stress_invariants` runtime builtin
// already produces — I1 ~ Pressure, I2 ~ Pressure², I3 ~ Pressure³ — using the
// `Pressure`/`Pressure2`/`Pressure3` aliases from units.ri. Task #6092.
//
// These were previously `Real` placeholders, but ONLY because Pa² and Pa³ had
// no named `.ri` alias to declare; that gap is closed, so the declaration now
// matches the runtime instead of erasing it. Do not revert to `Real`: user code
// can pass these fields where a `Pressure`/`Pressure2`/`Pressure3` is demanded,
// and `Real` breaks that at overload resolution.
//
// This deliberately DIVERGES from the `AnalysisResult` trait in analysis.ri,
// which KEEPS its `Real` placeholders. That is not an oversight to be tidied
// up: it is documented there as a structural contract that does not participate
// in dimension checking, and analysis.ri is `#no_prelude`, so it cannot see
// these aliases in the first place.
//
// Module placement: registered after `std.analysis` in `stdlib_loader.rs`
// so the growing prelude carries `Stress`/`AnalysisResult` when this module
// compiles. It now also REQUIRES `std.units` to precede it (see the ordering
// note on the `std.fea` entry in stdlib_loader.rs).

structure def StressInvariants {
    param i1 : Pressure
    param i2 : Pressure2
    param i3 : Pressure3
}
